'''Architeuthis''' (är-ki-tū′this), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀρχι-, first, chief, + τευθίς, squid.] A genus of
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Giant Squid (''Architeuthis dux''). (From Report of U. S. Fish Commission, 1884.)}}}}
monster cephalopods, or giant squids, of the family ''Ommastrephidæ'', and related to ''Ommastrephes'' except in size. {{smaller|Several species are described, as ''A. princeps'', ''A. harveyi'', and ''A. megaptera''. Some specimens are said to attain a total length of upward of 50 feet. These animals furnish the basis of fact for the fabulous monsters known as devil-fishes.}}
{{smaller block|One of the giant squids, belonging, doubtless, to the genus ''Architeuthis''. The whalers have long had accounts of the sperm whale eating giant squid, portions of the arms being vomited by these animals in their death flurry, but science has recognized the existence of these huge monsters for only a few years.
{{right|''Stand. Nat. Hist.'', I. 377.}}}}

'''architonnerre''' (är-shi-to-ner′), ''n.'' [F., < Gr. ἀρχι-, chief, + F. ''tonnerre'', thunder, thunderbolt, chamber (of a gun, etc.), < L. ''tonitrus'', thunder.] A form of steam-gun described by Leonardo da Vinci, and supposed to have been devised by Archimedes, which discharged iron balls with great noise.

